Question 25: Publication-Ready Dashboard Figure

AIM: Create a horizontal bar chart of class averages with target line and save figure.

Code:
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import os

subjects=['Maths','Physics','Programming','Chemistry','English']
class_avg=[72.3,65.1,81.4,58.9,76.0]
target=70

order=sorted(range(len(class_avg)),key=lambda i:class_avg[i])
subjects=[subjects[i] for i in order]
class_avg=[class_avg[i] for i in order]

plt.figure(figsize=(8,5))
colors=['red' if v<target else 'blue' for v in class_avg]
plt.barh(subjects,class_avg,color=colors)
plt.axvline(target,color='green',linestyle='--')
for i,v in enumerate(class_avg):
    plt.text(v+1,i,str(v))
plt.xlabel("Average")
plt.title("Subject Performance vs Target")

os.makedirs("task_4/figures",exist_ok=True)
plt.savefig("task_4/figures/subject_performance.png",dpi=300,bbox_inches='tight')
print("File saved:",os.path.exists("task_4/figures/subject_performance.png"))

Output:
File saved: True

Explanation:  
Functions used: barh(), axvline(), text(), savefig(), makedirs()
Usage: barh() to plot averages, axvline() to mark target, text() to label bars, savefig() to save figure, makedirs() to create folder
Interpretation: Chemistry (58.9) and Physics (65.1) fall below the target and need attention.
The colleague’s blank image happened because plt.show() clears the figure before saving. 
The correct order is to call savefig() before show().
